  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 153 PM CDT Sun Oct 4 2026\n\nSurface high pressure over the region this afternoon will shift \neastward into MN/WI by 12Z Monday. This will allow return flow \nsoutheast winds to develop overnight, along with increasing warm air \nadvection at 850mb. Because of this, lows are not expected to be \nquite as chilly as what was seen Sunday morning. Any readings in the \nupper 30s are expected to be confined to eastern portions of the CWA \nwhere winds will remain lightest the longest overnight. Lows tonight \ndid see an influence of colder-end percentiles over eastern areas \nafter seeing how NBM handled this morning's lows. Doing this though \ndid not bring an introduction of frost into the grids anywhere \nacross the CWA. \n\nWarm air advection continues into Monday with a tightening pressure \ngradient and passage of a warm front. South winds will gust from 25 \nto 35 mph across a good portion of the CWA on Monday as above normal \ntemperatures in the 70s to low 80s can be expected. Highs on Monday \nwere nudged more towards NBM90 values given the good mixing, mostly \nsunny skies, and warm air advection. Also dropped dewpoints a few \ndegrees from the inherited NBM, although this move did not produce \nRH values low enough to bring overly concerning fire danger.\n\nLower-level atmospheric winds (925mb) stay in the 30-40kt range \nMonday night across the eastern CWA and switch to the southwest. \nThis will favor a downslope regime across the Coteau region and have \nblended in a bit of NBM90 winds in the favored areas.\n\nCold front drops south through the region on Tuesday, but with not \nmuch cold air behind it as 850mb temps stay around +10C during the \nday Wednesday. Overall, temperatures still look to stay above normal \nthrough the upcoming week with highs in the upper 60s and 70s at \nleast through Saturday, with little to no precipitation expected.",
